# Rust Language Support

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:lux, path: "."}
])
```

Lux can run Prisms and Beams implemented in Rust. The `lux_core` crate in
`priv/rust` is compiled as a [Rustler](https://github.com/rusterlium/rustler)
NIF. Elixir terms are copied into owned Rust values before work starts, so
handlers never retain BEAM pointers. Failures come back as `{:error, reason}`
and do not panic across the FFI boundary.

## Why a NIF?

Python and Node adapters already exist. Rust is for work that should:

* stay memory-safe without a GC pause on the BEAM scheduler
* run CPU-heavy loops on a **dirty CPU scheduler**
* optionally `await` on a shared Tokio runtime without stalling a normal scheduler

## Check that the NIF loaded

```elixir
Lux.Rust.available?()
```

```elixir
Lux.Rust.version()
```

Primitive conversion smoke tests:

```elixir
Lux.Rust.add(40, 2)
```

```elixir
Lux.Rust.echo(%{"n" => 1})
```

```elixir
Lux.Rust.fib(10)
```

## Components

Rust Prisms and Beams register at NIF load. Elixir sees them as `%Lux.Prism{}`
/ `%Lux.Beam{}` whose `handler` is `{:rust, id}`. Lifecycle is explicit:

```
uninitialized -> ready -> running -> ready | stopped
```

`run/3` auto-initializes. Stopped components cannot be re-initialized in the
same VM.

```elixir
Lux.Rust.Component.list()
```

```elixir
{:ok, prism} = Lux.Rust.Component.view("echo")
prism.handler
```

```elixir
Lux.Rust.Component.run("echo", %{"hello" => "world"})
```

File-style ids resolve to the same component:

```elixir
Lux.Rust.Component.run("echo.rs", %{"n" => 42})
```

`Lux.Prism.run/2` dispatches `{:rust, id}` the same way it dispatches Python
and Node handlers:

```elixir
{:ok, prism} = Lux.Rust.Component.view("fib")
Lux.Prism.run(prism, 10)
```

## Built-in examples

| id | kind | input | output |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `echo` | prism | any convertible term | the same term |
| `hash` | prism | any convertible term | integer |
| `fib` | prism | integer or `%{"n" => n}` | Fibonacci(n), n <= 92 |
| `map_get` | prism | `%{"map" => map, "key" => key}` | value |
| `sleep` | prism (async) | `%{"ms" => n}` | `%{"ok" => true}` |
| `sequence` | beam | any convertible term | `hash` then `echo` |

Async handlers use a process-wide Tokio runtime. `component_run_async` is a
dirty CPU NIF so `block_on` cannot stall a normal scheduler.

```elixir
Lux.Rust.async_available?()
```

```elixir
if Lux.Rust.async_available?() do
  Lux.Rust.Component.run_async("sleep", %{"ms" => 10})
end
```

## Safety constraints

* No `unwrap` / `expect` / `panic!` on the NIF boundary.
* Integers use checked arithmetic; overflow is `{:error, {:runtime, _}}`.
* Unsupported BEAM terms (PIDs, refs, functions) fail conversion instead of
  being stored as raw pointers.
* Dirty CPU for Fibonacci, component `run`, and async `block_on`.

## Adding a component

Implement `Component` in `priv/rust/src/builtins.rs` (or a new module),
register it from `component::init_registry`, and call
`Lux.Rust.Component.run("your_id", input)` from Elixir. See
`examples/rust_components.exs`.
